  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In Young&rsquo;s double slit experiment, two slits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>&nbsp;distance apart and the separation from slits to screen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi></math> (as shown in figure). Now if two transparent slabs of equal thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> but refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>55</mn></math> are introduced in the path of beam <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#197;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively. The central bright fringe spot will shift by ________ number of fringes.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0063_875bee4a4e38.png\" style=\"width: 262px; height: 220px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "10",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0063_f5a4ccd042c4.png\" style=\"width: 396px; height: 260px;\" /></p><p>The change in path difference due to the two slabs at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mi>t</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>55</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>51</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>04</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;m</mi></math></p><p>Now, the&nbsp;distance of central maxima from geometrical center is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Now, fringe width,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Or,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Number of shift,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math>.</p>"
  }
}